let __v_180 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bundt tin\",\"bundt tins\",\"bundt pan\",\"bundt pans\",\"bundt\"]},\"A bundt tin is the ring-shaped cake tin with fluted sides. The hole up the middle is why the cake bakes evenly - there is no cold, heavy middle to sit raw while the edge dries - and the flutes give more crust. The catch is the shape: butter and flour every groove, tapping the tin as you go, and let the cake sit ten minutes before turning it out, or a third of the crust stays behind. A standard recipe fills a 10-cup (2.5-litre) tin.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ricer\",\"ricers\",\"potato ricer\",\"potato ricers\"]},\"A potato ricer is a press: you fill the hopper with cooked, dried potato and squeeze the handles, and the potato comes out through small holes in threads. That is what makes mash smooth without a machine - no lumps, and, unlike a blender or a food processor, it never overworks the starch, which is what turns mash gluey.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"muslin\",\"muslins\",\"muslin cloth\",\"muslin cloths\",\"cheesecloth\"]},\"Muslin is a loose woven cotton cloth, for straining and for holding things together: it lines a sieve to catch the fine bits in a stock or a jelly, wraps herbs and spices for a bouquet garni, and goes over a bowl of bread dough or a jug of milk as a cover that still lets air through. It is not the same as a tea towel - it is far looser, so liquid runs through it, and it can be boiled to clean it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mezzaluna\",\"mezzalunas\"]},\"A mezzaluna is a half-moon blade with a handle at each end, and you rock it back and forth across the board: for chopping herbs, nuts and garlic finely without a knife's stopping and starting. It is quick on a pile of parsley and hopeless on anything hard or wet.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"zester\",\"zesters\",\"microplane\",\"microplanes\",\"fine grater\"]},\"A zester takes the coloured outer peel off citrus without the white pith underneath, which is bitter. A microplane does the same job and also rasps hard cheese, nutmeg and garlic into fluff: one tool, a dozen uses. Take only the coloured layer - down to the pith is where the bitterness is.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"proving basket\",\"proving baskets\",\"banneton\",\"bannetons\",\"proving basket liner\"]},\"A proving basket - a banneton - is the bowl a shaped loaf sits in for its last rise. It holds the dough up as it spreads upwards instead of outwards, and its ridges leave the flour rings on the crust. Dust it heavily with flour (rice flour is best, it does not stick) or line it with a cloth; washed and wet, it goes mouldy.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"trivet\",\"trivets\",\"pan stand\",\"pan stands\"]},\"A trivet is the stand that keeps something hot off the table or the counter - a pan, a casserole, a slow cooker's pot. Wooden, cork, stone or metal, all it does is hold the heat away from the surface; a folded dry towel does the same job when the trivet is not there.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dutch oven\",\"dutch ovens\",\"cast iron pot\",\"cast iron casserole\",\"braiser\",\"braising pan\"]},\"A dutch oven is a heavy lidded pot - cast iron, or cast aluminium with enamel on it - that goes from the hob to the oven, which is the whole point of it: brown the meat on the burner, put the lid on, and let the oven do the next two hours. The thick walls hold heat and spread it evenly, so it braises without scorching, and the tight lid keeps the steam in. The enamelled ones want no metal utensils; bare cast iron wants seasoning and drying on the hob before it goes away.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"casserole dish\",\"casserole dishes\",\"oven dish\",\"ovenproof dish\",\"baking dish\",\"baking dishes\"]},\"A casserole dish is the wide, deep, lidded dish for cooking in the oven - stews, braises, a gratin - and it is chosen by what it is made of: enamelled cast iron holds and spreads heat for a long braise, stoneware is cheaper and slower to heat, and glass lets you see the browning but cannot go under a grill that is close to it. It should say ovenproof and give a top temperature; anything without that line stays off the hob and out of a hot oven.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pestle and mortar\",\"mortar and pestle\",\"pestle\",\"pestles\",\"mortar\",\"mortars\",\"pestle and mortars\"]},\"A pestle and mortar is a heavy bowl and a rounded club for crushing rather than cutting - spices, garlic, herbs, salt - which is why it gives a different, more fragrant result than a blade: the cell walls burst and the oils come out. Dry spices first, then anything wet, then wipe it out with salt and a cloth rather than washing it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ramekin\",\"ramekins\"]},\"A ramekin is a small straight-sided dish, for one portion of something baked or set - custard, a souffle, a fondant, potted shrimp, a dip on a plate. The straight sides matter: the contents slide out in one piece and a crust forms all the way up.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bench scraper\",\"bench scrapers\",\"dough scraper\",\"dough scrapers\",\"pastry scraper\",\"dough card\"]},\"A bench scraper is a flat rectangle of metal with a handle - a pastry chef's third hand. It lifts a sticky dough off the counter, cuts it into portions, scrapes dried flour and dough off the bench in one pass, and carries chopped vegetables to the pan.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fish slice\",\"fish slices\",\"slotted turner\",\"turner\"]},\"A fish slice is the wide, thin, usually slotted blade with a handle, for lifting things that fall apart - a whole fried fish, an egg, a slice of cake - and turning them without the corner of a spatula digging in. Thin and springy is what you want; a thick one is a burger press.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"spider skimmer\",\"spider skimmers\",\"kitchen spider\",\"spider for chips\",\"skimmer\",\"skimmers\",\"spiders for chips\"]},\"A spider is the wide, shallow wire-mesh basket on a long handle. It lifts food out of boiling water or hot oil - fried chips, blanched greens, a poached egg, a handful of pasta to test - and lets the water or oil run straight back into the pan, without dragging the pan off the heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"skillet\",\"skillets\"]},\"A skillet is a frying pan with straight, deeper sides and usually an ovenproof handle, built to take high heat: searing, frying, then going into the oven to finish. In Britain the word is used loosely for a heavy frying pan; the straight side is the part that matters, because it holds the fat and the food in.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"slotted spoon\",\"slotted spoons\"]},\"A slotted spoon has holes or slots in the bowl, for lifting the solid part out of a liquid and letting the liquid drain back: vegetables out of a pan, olives out of a jar, a poached egg out of the water, skimming the scum off a stock.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"gratin dish\",\"gratin dishes\"]},\"A gratin dish is a shallow, wide ovenproof dish - the opposite of a deep casserole. The point is the exposed surface: a shallow layer means more of the top browns and crisps, which is what a gratin is. A roasting tin will do at a push, if it is deep enough to hold the sauce.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"springform tin\",\"springform tins\",\"spring form tin\",\"springform pan\",\"springform\"]},\"A springform tin has a clip on the side: undo it and the ring lifts away from the cake, so a cheesecake or a torte goes out of the tin without being turned upside down. Line the base or the leaky ones drip, and run a knife round the inside before you release the clip, which stops a set top cracking.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"piping bag\",\"piping bags\",\"icing bag\"]},\"A piping bag is the cone you fill with icing, cream, choux or meringue mix and squeeze out through a nozzle, so it comes out in the shape you want - a rosette, a line, a swirl. A disposable bag plus a star nozzle covers most of it; a sandwich bag with the corner snipped off does for a swirl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"palette knife\",\"palette knives\",\"offset spatula\"]},\"A palette knife is the long flexible blunt blade with no edge, for lifting, spreading and levelling rather than cutting: icing a cake flat, lifting a slice off the board, sliding under a fish to turn it. An offset one - the blade stepped down from the handle - is for spreading inside a tin.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sieve\",\"sieves\",\"fine sieve\",\"flour sieve\"]},\"A sieve is the round mesh for flour, icing sugar, cocoa and custard: it breaks up lumps and puts air in, which is why a sponge recipe sifts. The mesh is fine, so it also strains small quantities of liquid and seeds; for a big pan of pasta you want a colander, whose holes are large enough for the water to leave in seconds.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"stick blender\",\"stick blenders\",\"immersion blender\",\"hand blender\",\"hand blenders\"]},\"A stick blender is the wand you put straight into the pan or jug: soup, sauce, a smoothie, all blended where they are, with nothing to pour and one thing to wash. Keep the blade under the surface before you switch on or it spatters, and do not use it on boiling soup in a deep pan without taking the pan off the heat first.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"grater\",\"graters\",\"box grater\",\"cheese grater\"]},\"A grater turns hard food into shreds: cheese, carrot, apple, potato, courgette, nutmeg. A box grater is the four-sided one - coarse for cheese and vegetables, fine for parmesan and zest, and a slicer on one side; a flat or rotary grater is for cheese at the table.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"spatula\",\"spatulas\"]},\"A spatula is the flat, flexible, blunt tool for scraping and folding - getting every bit of cake mix out of the bowl, folding egg whites in without knocking the air out, easing a fried egg off the pan. A silicone one will not scratch a non-stick pan; the metal sort that turns and lifts food is the fish slice.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ladle\",\"ladles\"]},\"A ladle is the deep, long-handled spoon with a lip, for moving liquid in useful amounts: soup into bowls, stock into a jug, sauce over a plate. A 100ml ladle is about three tablespoons, which is a handy measure when a recipe says a ladleful.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"saucepan\",\"saucepans\"]},\"A saucepan is the deep, round pan with a long handle, for anything with liquid in it - sauces, rice, pasta, boiled eggs, milk. Size is the thing people get wrong: a small pan boils over and a big one scorches a small amount, so 16-18cm for one or two people and 20cm for a family.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"roasting tin\",\"roasting tins\",\"roasting tray\",\"roasting dish\"]},\"A roasting tin is the deep-sided metal tin that goes in the oven for joints, roast vegetables and traybakes. Metal, not glass, for high heat and for browning; leave a gap between the pieces so they roast rather than steam, and put the tin in the oven while it heats for crisper potatoes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"thermometer\",\"thermometers\",\"meat thermometer\",\"meat thermometers\",\"sugar thermometer\",\"jam thermometer\",\"food thermometer\",\"probe thermometer\"]},\"A cooking thermometer takes the guesswork out of doneness: 75C in the middle of poultry, 71C for pork and minced meat, 63C for a beef joint eaten pink, and 104-105C for jam at setting point. Take the meat out of the oven to check it, or the reading climbs as the probe goes in, and leave it in for ten seconds rather than a moment.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"kitchen scale\",\"kitchen scales\",\"weighing scales\",\"digital scales\",\"food scale\"]},\"A kitchen scale weighs in grams, which is how most baking is written and the only way to be sure of flour, butter and sugar - a cup of flour varies by a third depending on how it is scooped. Put the bowl on, set it to zero, and add each thing in turn.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"blender\",\"blenders\",\"jug blender\"]},\"A jug blender is the tall jug with a blade in the base, for smoothies, soups, hummus and crushing ice: liquid in, smooth out. It needs enough liquid to keep the food moving, so a thick mix wants stirring or a little water; hot soup goes in a third at a time, with the lid held down, because steam lifts it off.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"carving fork\",\"carving forks\",\"meat fork\"]},\"A carving fork is the long two-pronged fork that holds the joint still while you cut it, so your fingers are not near the blade. The long prongs go in once, at the thick end, and the guard stops the knife sliding up towards your hand.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"potato masher\",\"potato mashers\",\"masher\",\"mashers\"]},\"A potato masher is the wavy wire or plate on a handle, for crushing cooked potato, swede, carrot or beans straight in the pan. It leaves a rougher mash than a ricer, which is fine for most things and wrong for a smooth one: add the butter and milk warm, and mash while the potato is hot.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cooling rack\",\"cooling racks\",\"wire rack\",\"wire racks\",\"cake rack\"]},\"A cooling rack is the wire grid that lets air under a cake or a tray of biscuits as they cool, so the base does not go damp and soggy on the worktop. Leave the bake in its tin for ten minutes first, then move it to the rack; a rack upside down over the tin turns a cake out without breaking it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"stockpot\",\"stockpots\",\"stock pot\",\"stock pans\"]},\"A stockpot is the tall, deep pan for a lot of liquid over a long time: stock, a big pan of pasta, a ham hock, a batch of soup. Tall and narrow is the point - less surface means less evaporation - which is the opposite of a wide saute pan.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"saute pan\",\"saute pans\",\"sauté pan\",\"sauteuse\"]},\"A saute pan has straight, fairly high sides and a wide flat base: enough floor to brown without crowding, enough wall to hold a sauce or a braise. It is the pan for a risotto, a curry or a chicken joint browned and then finished in the oven.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"crepe pan\",\"crepe pans\",\"pancake pan\"]},\"A crepe pan is a shallow pan with a very low lip and a flat base, so a ladle of batter can be swirled right to the edge instead of running up the side. A good non-stick frying pan of about 20cm does the same job; the thin lip is what makes it easier to get a palette knife under a crepe.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"griddle\",\"griddles\",\"griddle pan\",\"flat griddle\"]},\"A griddle is the thick flat plate - a pan, or a slab over the hob - for cooking with almost no fat and no side walls: pancakes, flatbreads, bacon, a steak. It gets very hot and stays hot, so things brown rather than steam; a ridged one leaves the dark lines and lets the fat run away.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pastry brush\",\"pastry brushes\",\"basting brush\"]},\"A pastry brush is the small brush for glazing and greasing: beaten egg on a pie lid, milk on a bun, melted butter in a tin, a wash of syrup on a warm cake. Silicone takes the heat and washes clean; a bristle brush gives a finer finish but sheds, so keep it for cool jobs.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"food mill\",\"food mills\",\"mouli\",\"moulinette\"]},\"A food mill is the pan with a crank and a perforated disc underneath, for pureeing and sieving in one go: it pushes cooked apple, tomato or potato through the holes and leaves the skins and seeds behind. Swapping the disc sets how fine it comes out, which is why it makes smoother mash and seedless sauce without a blender.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"colander\",\"colanders\",\"sieve colander\"]},\"A colander is the bowl with holes in it, for draining the water off food - pasta, potatoes, rice, berries - which is quicker than lifting them out a spoonful at a time. Its holes are large, so a big pan of pasta water leaves in seconds and a custard runs straight through; the fine-mesh one for flour and icing sugar is a sieve. A metal colander takes boiling water straight from the pan, a plastic one is fine for cold rinsing but softens and stains under a full pan, and the ones with a single handle are for lifting it out of the sink.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mandoline\",\"mandolines\",\"mandolin slicer\",\"v slicer\"]},\"A mandoline is a flat board with a blade set at an adjustable height, for slices of even thickness - 2mm potato for dauphinoise, cabbage for slaw - which a knife cannot match for speed. It is also the sharpest thing in most kitchens: use the hand guard, or a cut glove, every time, and stop while there is still a knuckle's width of vegetable left. Wash it straight after, since dried starch glues the blade in place and the next slice slips.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bain marie\",\"bain-marie\",\"water bath\"]},\"A bain marie is a pan of simmering water with a bowl sitting in it, so what is in the bowl is heated by steam and never by flame: chocolate melts without seizing, custard and hollandaise thicken without curdling, and a sauce stays warm without catching. The bowl must not touch the water and the water must not boil hard - steam is gentle, a rolling boil splashes and scorches. A heatproof bowl over a saucepan does the same job, and a folded towel under the bowl stops it rattling.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"balloon whisk\",\"balloon whisks\",\"whisk\",\"whisks\",\"wire whisk\"]},\"A balloon whisk is the round, balloon-shaped wire one, for putting air in: egg whites to peaks, cream to soft folds, a batter smooth. Its wide loops move a lot of air, which a flat or a ball whisk does not, so it is the one for meringue; it is no good for a thick mixture - that is a wooden spoon's job. Use a big bowl and lift the whisk right out of the mixture each turn, and take the cream from the fridge cold.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pantry\",\"pantries\",\"larder\",\"larders\"]},\"A pantry or larder is a cool, dark, dry cupboard for the food that keeps: tins, dried pasta and rice, flour, sugar, oil, spices. Cool and dry matter more than size - a shelf by a radiator or a sunny window turns flour and rice stale and brings weevils - so put it on an outside wall away from the cooker. Keep the oldest at the front of the shelf and the new shopping behind it, and write the date on anything you decant into a jar.\"]}]}")?;
